"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer keeps the two measures separate and correct. It defines tracking error as the standard deviation of daily fund-minus-benchmark returns over the agreed one-year window. It defines tracking difference as the cumulative return gap. Adding √252 annualization is a reasonable convention, not an error. Placing fee drag under tracking difference fits the grader note: it never claims a constant daily fee raises the standard deviation of daily active returns. It covers most central causes: sampling/optimization versus full replication and active weights, cash drag/buffers, index reconstitutions/rebalances and corporate actions, pricing and closing-price timing, fair value, stale prices, dividend accrual/withholding, trading costs and timing, and creation/redemption flows. 'Caps' and 'liquidity-constrained holdings' partly cover diversification limits. Minor omissions: variable expenses are not named as a source of variability. It does not explicitly say that a constant fee shifts the mean but not the variance. It does not explicitly say the benchmark and measurement method must be agreed before diagnosis. FX items are largely incidental for a US equity index ETF but not wrong. No material error.\"}","ttft_ms":3330,"type":"result","duration_ms":6837,"uuid":"9c42cad7-764f-4502-b908-14e351573408","ttft_stream_ms":863,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":864,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
